-> Domain constraints = Z (integers), R (reals), N (naturals).
x in Z
x = 42
assert x = 42

y in R
y = 3
assert y = 3

z in N
z = 7
assert z = 7

-> Range narrowing on an integer domain collapses to the unique integer
-> satisfying all constraints — same result as pinning it directly.
a in Z
a < 2
a > 0
assert a = 1

-> `∈ Z/R/N` and `in Z/R/N` are the same operator, just different notation.
c ∈ Z
c < 2
c > 0
assert c = 1

-> Naturals exclude negatives.
n ∈ N
n < 1
assert n = 0
